---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert creator operations strategist, content production manager and AI-assisted workflow planner.

TASK:
Build a practical creator workflow from idea generation through publishing, repurposing and performance review.

The workflow should help the creator:
- reduce wasted time
- separate creative work from admin work
- batch similar tasks
- use AI where it genuinely saves effort
- protect quality and factual accuracy
- avoid repetitive manual steps
- publish consistently without overloading the creator
- maintain a clear review process
- learn from content performance over time

The workflow should be realistic for the creator's actual time, tools, team size and publishing capacity.

CREATOR / BRAND:
[Name and short description.]

NICHE:
[Main topic or industry.]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who is the content for?]

PRIMARY PLATFORMS:
[Instagram / Facebook / TikTok / YouTube / LinkedIn / blog / email / other.]

CONTENT FORMATS:
[Short video / long-form video / carousel / image post / article / email / podcast / other.]

POSTING FREQUENCY:
[For example: 3 posts per week.]

AVAILABLE TIME:
[Hours per day or week.]

TEAM SIZE:
[Solo / assistant / editor / designer / agency / other.]

TOOLS AVAILABLE:
[Phone / camera / Canva / CapCut / Adobe / Notion / Google Drive / AI tools / spreadsheet / other.]

AI TOOLS AVAILABLE:
[List if known.]

CURRENT WORKFLOW:
[Describe the current process if one exists.]

CURRENT PROBLEMS:
[For example: too much time editing, no idea system, inconsistent posting, duplicated work, missed deadlines.]

CONTENT GOALS:
[Growth / authority / leads / sales / community / education / monetization / consistency / other.]

RESEARCH NEEDS:
[Low / medium / high.]

APPROVAL REQUIREMENTS:
[None / self-review / client approval / legal review / manager review / other.]

BRAND OR QUALITY REQUIREMENTS:
[Optional.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

WORKFLOW DESIGN REQUIREMENTS:

1. START WITH REAL CAPACITY

2. DO NOT CREATE A WORKFLOW THAT REQUIRES MORE TIME THAN THE CREATOR HAS

3. CALCULATE A PRACTICAL WEEKLY PRODUCTION LOAD

4. DISTINGUISH BETWEEN:
Creative work
Research
Production
Editing
Publishing
Admin
Analytics

5. IDENTIFY REPEATED TASKS

6. IDENTIFY TASKS THAT CAN BE BATCHED

7. IDENTIFY TASKS THAT SHOULD NOT BE BATCHED

8. CREATE A CLEAR PIPELINE

9. DEFAULT PIPELINE:
Idea
Research
Validation
Planning
Drafting
Production
Editing
Review
Scheduling
Publishing
Repurposing
Performance review

10. ADAPT THE PIPELINE TO THE CREATOR'S FORMAT

11. DO NOT FORCE VIDEO-SPECIFIC STEPS ON TEXT-ONLY CREATORS

12. DO NOT FORCE LONG-FORM STEPS ON SHORT-FORM-ONLY CREATORS

13. CREATE AN IDEA CAPTURE SYSTEM

14. THE IDEA SYSTEM SHOULD CAPTURE:
Topic
Audience problem
Content pillar
Format
Platform
Objective
Status

15. KEEP IDEA CAPTURE SIMPLE

16. DO NOT CREATE A COMPLEX DATABASE UNLESS NEEDED

17. CREATE AN IDEA REVIEW PROCESS

18. SEPARATE:
Good idea
Needs research
Duplicate
Not relevant
Future idea

19. CREATE A PRIORITIZATION METHOD

20. PRIORITIZE BASED ON:
Audience value
Strategic fit
Creator credibility
Effort
Timeliness
Repurposing potential

21. DO NOT USE FALSE PRECISION

22. USE:
High
Medium
Low

when scoring is helpful.

23. CREATE A RESEARCH STEP

24. RESEARCH SHOULD MATCH CONTENT RISK

25. LOW-RISK CONTENT MAY REQUIRE:
Basic verification

26. HIGHER-RISK CONTENT MAY REQUIRE:
Primary sources
Current sources
Expert review

27. FLAG CONTENT INVOLVING:
Health
Finance
Legal
Safety
Current events
Statistics
Platform updates
Product specifications

28. DO NOT LET AI GENERATE UNSOURCED FACTS

29. CREATE A SOURCE-CAPTURE METHOD

30. STORE:
Source link
Source date
Key fact
Claim supported

31. DO NOT OVERCOMPLICATE SOURCE MANAGEMENT

32. CREATE A CONTENT BRIEF

33. BRIEF SHOULD INCLUDE:
Topic
Audience
Objective
Hook
Key points
Format
CTA
Sources
Visual needs

34. KEEP THE BRIEF SHORT ENOUGH TO USE

35. CREATE A SCRIPT / DRAFT STEP

36. DEFINE WHEN AI SHOULD HELP

37. AI MAY HELP WITH:
Idea expansion
Outline
First draft
Hook variations
Caption variations
Repurposing
Summaries
Checklists
Formatting

38. AI SHOULD NOT BE TRUSTED BLINDLY FOR:
Facts
Statistics
Current information
Legal advice
Health claims
Financial claims
Direct quotes
Source attribution

39. CREATE A HUMAN REVIEW STEP

40. REVIEW:
Accuracy
Brand voice
Clarity
Repetition
Claims
CTA
Platform fit

41. CREATE A PRODUCTION STEP

42. FOR VIDEO INCLUDE WHEN RELEVANT:
Shot list
Props
Lighting
Audio
B-roll
Screen recordings
On-screen text

43. FOR STATIC CONTENT INCLUDE WHEN RELEVANT:
Layout
Image selection
Graphic preparation
Alt text
Caption

44. FOR ARTICLES INCLUDE WHEN RELEVANT:
Outline
Sources
Draft
Editing
Internal links
Metadata

45. CREATE A BATCHING PLAN

46. GROUP SIMILAR TASKS

47. POSSIBLE BATCHES:
Research
Script writing
Recording
Editing
Thumbnail creation
Caption writing
Scheduling

48. DO NOT BATCH TASKS THAT REQUIRE FRESH CONTEXT IF THAT HARMS QUALITY

49. CREATE A PRODUCTION DAY MODEL

50. EXAMPLE:
Monday — plan
Tuesday — script
Wednesday — record
Thursday — edit
Friday — schedule and review

51. ADAPT TO AVAILABLE TIME

52. CREATE A DAILY MICRO-WORKFLOW IF THE CREATOR HAS LIMITED TIME

53. EXAMPLE:
30 minutes — idea/research
45 minutes — draft
45 minutes — production

54. DO NOT ASSUME THE CREATOR CAN WORK EVERY DAY

55. CREATE A FILE ORGANIZATION SYSTEM

56. KEEP IT SIMPLE

57. RECOMMENDED STRUCTURE:
Ideas
Research
Drafts
Raw media
Edits
Published
Repurposing

58. CREATE FILE-NAMING RULES

59. FILE NAMES SHOULD INCLUDE:
Date
Topic
Format
Version

60. DO NOT CREATE OVERLY LONG FILE NAMES

61. CREATE VERSION CONTROL

62. USE SIMPLE STATES:
Draft
Review
Approved
Scheduled
Published

63. CREATE A CONTENT STATUS SYSTEM

64. POSSIBLE STATUS:
Idea
Researching
Planned
Drafted
Recorded
Editing
Review
Scheduled
Published
Repurposed

65. DO NOT CREATE TOO MANY STATUS STATES

66. CREATE AN APPROVAL FLOW

67. IF SOLO:
Self-review checklist

68. IF CLIENT / TEAM:
Define responsible reviewer

69. CREATE A PUBLISHING CHECKLIST

70. CHECK:
Caption
Thumbnail
Title
Links
CTA
Tags
Subtitles
Accessibility
Scheduling
Sources

71. DO NOT ASSUME EVERY PLATFORM NEEDS ALL ITEMS

72. CREATE A REPURPOSING STEP

73. IDENTIFY SOURCE CONTENT

74. TURN ONE STRONG SOURCE ASSET INTO:
Short video
Carousel
Post
Email
Article
Clip
Quote graphic

75. DO NOT SIMPLY COPY-PASTE THE SAME CONTENT

76. ADAPT EACH ASSET TO THE PLATFORM

77. CREATE A REPURPOSING DECISION RULE

78. REPURPOSE WHEN:
Topic performed well
Topic remains relevant
Content has multiple useful angles
Long-form source contains standalone insights

79. DO NOT REPURPOSE WEAK CONTENT AUTOMATICALLY

80. CREATE A CONTENT ARCHIVE

81. TRACK:
Published date
Platform
Topic
Pillar
Format
Link
Performance notes

82. KEEP ANALYTICS TRACKING PRACTICAL

83. CREATE A PERFORMANCE REVIEW PROCESS

84. REVIEW CONTENT BY:
Objective
Platform
Pillar
Format

85. DO NOT JUDGE EVERYTHING ONLY BY VIEWS

86. POSSIBLE METRICS:
Reach
Watch time
Retention
Saves
Shares
Comments
Clicks
Leads
Conversions

87. MATCH METRIC TO OBJECTIVE

88. CREATE A WEEKLY REVIEW

89. WEEKLY REVIEW SHOULD ANSWER:
What was published?
What worked?
What underperformed?
What questions appeared?
What should be reused?

90. CREATE A MONTHLY REVIEW

91. MONTHLY REVIEW SHOULD ANSWER:
Which pillars are strongest?
Which formats are sustainable?
Which topics should be repeated?
Which tasks consume too much time?
Which workflow step needs improvement?

92. CREATE A CONTENT FEEDBACK LOOP

93. PERFORMANCE SHOULD INFORM:
Future ideas
Hooks
Formats
Pillars
Posting frequency

94. DO NOT CHASE EVERY SHORT-TERM PERFORMANCE CHANGE

95. CREATE A BACKLOG SYSTEM

96. KEEP:
Ready ideas
Needs research
Seasonal
Evergreen
Experimental

97. CREATE EVERGREEN CONTENT PROCESS

98. EVERGREEN CONTENT SHOULD BE REVIEWED PERIODICALLY

99. FLAG OUTDATED INFORMATION

100. CREATE TIME-SENSITIVE CONTENT PROCESS

101. TIME-SENSITIVE CONTENT NEEDS:
Faster research
Current verification
Clear publishing deadline

102. DO NOT PUBLISH STALE CURRENT-EVENT CONTENT

103. CREATE A TEMPLATE LIBRARY

104. TEMPLATES MAY INCLUDE:
Video brief
Script outline
Caption structure
Thumbnail brief
Research checklist
Publishing checklist

105. DO NOT OVER-TEMPLATE CREATIVE WORK

106. CREATE REUSABLE PROMPT SYSTEMS

107. MAP AI PROMPTS TO WORKFLOW STAGES

108. EXAMPLE:
Idea stage → Short-Form Video Idea Generator
Hook stage → Hook Generator
Outline stage → YouTube Video Outline
Caption stage → Caption Writer
Repurposing stage → Content Repurposing

109. DO NOT USE AI FOR EVERY STEP JUST BECAUSE IT IS AVAILABLE

110. CREATE AN AI REVIEW RULE

111. EVERY AI OUTPUT SHOULD BE CHECKED FOR:
Facts
Tone
Originality
Repetition
Unsupported claims
Formatting

112. CREATE A QUALITY GATE

113. CONTENT CANNOT MOVE TO PUBLISHING UNTIL IT PASSES:
Accuracy
Clarity
Brand fit
Platform fit
CTA fit

114. CREATE A MINIMUM VIABLE WORKFLOW

115. IF THE CREATOR IS OVERWHELMED
Reduce the workflow to:
Plan
Create
Review
Publish
Learn

116. CREATE AN ADVANCED WORKFLOW ONLY WHEN CAPACITY SUPPORTS IT

117. IDENTIFY BOTTLENECKS

118. POSSIBLE BOTTLENECKS:
Research
Scripting
Recording
Editing
Design
Approval
Scheduling

119. FOR EACH BOTTLENECK
Suggest:
Simplify
Batch
Template
Delegate
Automate
Remove

120. DO NOT AUTOMATE A BAD PROCESS BEFORE SIMPLIFYING IT

121. CREATE AUTOMATION OPPORTUNITIES

122. POSSIBLE LOW-RISK AUTOMATION:
File naming
Status updates
Reminder systems
Calendar scheduling
Template duplication
Analytics collection

123. DO NOT AUTOMATE FINAL FACT APPROVAL

124. DO NOT AUTOMATE SENSITIVE CLAIM APPROVAL

125. CREATE DELEGATION OPPORTUNITIES

126. IF TEAM EXISTS
Separate:
Creator-only tasks
Assistant tasks
Editor tasks
Designer tasks

127. KEEP CREATIVE OWNERSHIP CLEAR

128. CREATE STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURES FOR REPEATED TASKS

129. PRIORITIZE SOPs FOR:
Publishing
File handling
Editing handoff
Research
Approvals

130. DO NOT DOCUMENT ONE-OFF TASKS AS FULL SOPs

131. CREATE A CONTENT CALENDAR CONNECTION

132. THE CALENDAR SHOULD SHOW:
Publish date
Platform
Format
Pillar
Status

133. DO NOT OVERLOAD THE CALENDAR WITH INTERNAL DETAILS

134. CREATE BUFFER TIME

135. INCLUDE SPACE FOR:
Delays
Reshoots
Current events
Unexpected workload

136. DO NOT SCHEDULE 100% OF AVAILABLE TIME

137. CREATE A REALISTIC CONTENT BUFFER

138. EXAMPLE:
Keep at least one ready-to-publish evergreen piece when practical.

139. CREATE A BURNOUT-PREVENTION RULE

140. REDUCE:
Unnecessary platforms
Excessive posting frequency
Overediting
Constant trend chasing

141. PRIORITIZE:
Consistency
Quality
Repeatable systems

142. CREATE A PLATFORM-SPECIFIC WORKFLOW

143. DO NOT ASSUME ONE MASTER FILE CAN ALWAYS BE PUBLISHED EVERYWHERE UNCHANGED

144. CREATE A FINAL ADAPTATION STEP

145. REVIEW:
Aspect ratio
Caption
Title
Duration
Thumbnail
CTA
Hashtags
Formatting

146. CREATE ACCESSIBILITY CHECKS

147. WHEN RELEVANT CHECK:
Captions/subtitles
Readable text
Contrast
Alt text

148. CREATE A REVIEW ARCHIVE

149. KEEP LEARNINGS FROM:
Successful content
Failed experiments
Audience comments
Production issues

150. CREATE A CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT LOOP

151. EACH MONTH CHOOSE:
One workflow step to improve

152. DO NOT CHANGE THE ENTIRE SYSTEM AT ONCE

153. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- workflow matches available time
- workload is realistic
- responsibilities are clear
- research requirements are appropriate
- AI use is purposeful
- human review is preserved
- batching opportunities are used
- unnecessary steps are removed
- publishing checklist exists
- repurposing is platform-aware
- analytics match objectives
- feedback informs future content
- bottlenecks are identified
- automation does not replace critical judgment
- workflow can be repeated consistently
- creator has a simpler fallback workflow for busy periods

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. CREATOR WORKFLOW SNAPSHOT
Provide:
Platforms
Formats
Posting frequency
Available time
Team size
Main constraints
Main workflow problem

2. RECOMMENDED WORKFLOW
Show the full pipeline from idea to performance review.

3. WORKFLOW STAGES

For each stage provide:
Purpose
Tasks
Owner
Tools
AI use
Human review
Output
Status

4. WEEKLY PRODUCTION PLAN
Create a realistic weekly schedule.

5. DAILY MICRO-WORKFLOW
Create a shorter version for limited-time days.

6. BATCHING PLAN
Show:
Tasks to batch
Recommended batch frequency
Expected benefit

7. IDEA MANAGEMENT SYSTEM
Provide:
Fields
Statuses
Review process

8. RESEARCH WORKFLOW
Provide:
Verification level
Source capture
Fact-check rule

9. CONTENT BRIEF TEMPLATE
Create a concise reusable template.

10. AI WORKFLOW MAP
Show which AI prompt or tool should be used at each useful stage.

11. HUMAN REVIEW CHECKPOINTS
Identify where human judgment is mandatory.

12. PRODUCTION WORKFLOW
Adapt to the creator's formats.

13. FILE & FOLDER SYSTEM
Provide a simple structure and naming convention.

14. STATUS SYSTEM
Recommend the minimum useful workflow statuses.

15. PUBLISHING CHECKLIST

16. REPURPOSING WORKFLOW
Explain how published source content becomes platform-native secondary assets.

17. CONTENT ARCHIVE
Define what should be tracked after publishing.

18. WEEKLY REVIEW
Provide a short review checklist.

19. MONTHLY REVIEW
Provide a deeper performance and workflow review.

20. BOTTLENECK ANALYSIS
For each bottleneck provide:
Problem
Cause
Recommended fix

21. AUTOMATION OPPORTUNITIES
Separate:
Safe to automate
Needs human review
Do not automate

22. DELEGATION PLAN
If relevant.

23. TEMPLATE LIBRARY
Recommend reusable templates.

24. BUSY-WEEK FALLBACK WORKFLOW
Create a minimum viable system for weeks with limited time.

25. CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT PLAN
Recommend one workflow improvement cycle.

26. FINAL RECOMMENDATION
Provide:
Most important workflow change
Best batching opportunity
Best AI-assisted step
Most important human review point
Most likely bottleneck
Best first automation or template

IMPORTANT:
- Build for the creator's actual capacity.
- Simplify before automating.
- Use AI to reduce repetitive work, not to replace factual or editorial judgment.
- Keep quality and credibility checks before publishing.
- Make the workflow repeatable rather than unnecessarily complex.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Map the full content pipeline",
    description:
      "Define what happens from idea capture through research, creation, review, publishing, repurposing and performance analysis so content does not disappear between disconnected tasks.",
  },
  {
    title: "Batch repetitive work",
    description:
      "Group research, scripting, recording, editing and scheduling where appropriate so the creator spends less time constantly switching between different types of work.",
  },
  {
    title: "Use AI selectively",
    description:
      "Apply AI to idea expansion, outlines, captions, repurposing and repetitive drafting while preserving human review for facts, judgment, brand voice and important claims.",
  },
  {
    title: "Build a feedback loop",
    description:
      "Use weekly and monthly performance reviews to improve future topics, formats and workflow decisions instead of treating publishing as the end of the process.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Creator: Solo professional content creator.",
    "Niche: Warehouse, inventory and operations.",
    "Platforms: LinkedIn, Facebook and YouTube Shorts.",
    "Posting frequency: Three pieces per week.",
    "Available time: Around 10 hours per week.",
    "Tools: Phone, Canva, CapCut, Google Drive and AI tools.",
    "Main problem: Too much time is lost creating each post from scratch.",
  ],

  output: [
    "MONDAY — Plan and research three content ideas from the existing content pillars.",
    "TUESDAY — Create hooks and short briefs for all three pieces in one batch.",
    "WEDNESDAY — Record the week's video content in one production session.",
    "THURSDAY — Edit videos and prepare graphics, captions and thumbnails.",
    "FRIDAY — Final fact check, schedule content and identify one repurposing opportunity.",
    "AI USE — Use AI for idea expansion, hook options, outlines, captions and repurposing. Keep final claims, examples and technical accuracy under human review.",
    "ARCHIVE — Record platform, topic, pillar, format, publish date and performance notes after publishing.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Simplify before you automate",
    description:
      "If a workflow has unnecessary steps, automation usually makes the bad process run faster. Remove waste first, then automate stable repetitive tasks.",
  },
  {
    title: "Batch by type of work",
    description:
      "Writing three scripts together or recording several videos in one session usually creates less context switching than finishing one post completely before starting the next.",
  },
  {
    title: "Publishing is not the final step",
    description:
      "Archive useful content, review performance and capture audience questions so each published piece makes the next content cycle easier.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Creator Workflow Planner Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Build a practical creator workflow for ideas, research, content production, AI-assisted drafting, publishing, repurposing and performance review."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="CREATORS"
    title="Creator Workflow Planner"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Build a repeatable creator workflow from idea capture to publishing, repurposing and performance review, with practical batching and selective AI assistance."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Creator workflow",
      "Content operations",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Solo creators",
      "Content teams",
      "Multi-platform creators",
      "AI-assisted workflows",
      "Batch production",
      "Content operations",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Turn content creation into a repeatable system."
    introDescription="Map each stage of the creator workflow, remove unnecessary work, batch repetitive tasks and use AI where it saves time without sacrificing accuracy or editorial judgment."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Build a manageable weekly workflow for a solo professional creator."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="A sustainable system beats a complicated one."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Connect the whole Creator toolkit"
    sidebarDescription="Combine KRIYANO's Content Pillar Builder, Audience Question Generator, Hook Generator, YouTube Video Outline, Caption Writer and Content Repurposing prompts inside one repeatable workflow."
    warningTitle="Keep human review in the workflow"
    warningText="AI can accelerate planning, drafting and repurposing, but factual accuracy, sensitive claims, brand judgment and final publishing decisions should still be reviewed by a responsible person."
  />
</BaseLayout>